@page "/research"

<PageTitle>Research Report | SmartX</PageTitle>

<div class="research-page">

    <section class="research-hero">
        <div>
            <span class="research-label">PROG7312 Research Report</span>
            <h1>SmartX Application Research</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                Exploring application telemetry, monitoring, security and the use of
                modern web technologies within the SmartX application.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>1. Introduction</h2>

        <p>
            Modern software applications require more than functional user interfaces
            and business logic. Developers must also consider application performance,
            reliability, security and the ability to understand how users interact with
            the system. One approach to achieving this is through application telemetry,
            which involves collecting operational information such as requests, errors,
            performance measurements and application events.
        </p>

        <p>
            SmartX was developed using ASP.NET Core and Blazor. Blazor provides a
            component-based approach for developing interactive web applications using
            C# and .NET rather than requiring JavaScript for the majority of application
            logic (Microsoft, 2026a). This makes it suitable for integrating application
            features, monitoring functionality and reusable user-interface components
            within a single .NET ecosystem.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>2. Application Telemetry</h2>

        <p>
            Telemetry refers to information that an application records and sends to a
            monitoring platform so that developers can understand the behaviour and
            health of the system. Typical telemetry information includes application
            requests, response times, exceptions, dependencies, logs and custom events.
        </p>

        <p>
            Microsoft Application Insights supports standard, log-based and custom
            metrics. Custom telemetry is particularly useful when developers need to
            monitor information that is specific to the application's requirements
            (Microsoft, 2026b). In SmartX, telemetry can therefore be used to record
            important application activities and provide information that would help
            identify failures or performance problems.
        </p>

        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>Examples of SmartX Telemetry</h3>

            <div class="telemetry-grid">
                <div class="telemetry-item">
                    <strong>Requests</strong>
                    <span>Tracks interactions with the application.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="telemetry-item">
                    <strong>Performance</strong>
                    <span>Measures how quickly operations are completed.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="telemetry-item">
                    <strong>Exceptions</strong>
                    <span>Identifies application errors and failures.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="telemetry-item">
                    <strong>Custom Events</strong>
                    <span>Records important SmartX-specific actions.</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>3. Benefits of Monitoring</h2>

        <p>
            Monitoring provides developers with visibility into an application after
            deployment. Instead of relying only on user reports, telemetry allows
            technical teams to observe behaviour and investigate problems using
            collected information.
        </p>

        <p>
            Application Insights can automatically collect traces, metrics, logs and
            exceptions through OpenTelemetry instrumentation (Microsoft, 2026b).
            This supports proactive application maintenance because failures,
            performance degradation and unusual activity can be identified from the
            telemetry generated by the system.
        </p>

        <div class="benefit-list">
            <div>
                <span class="number">01</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Performance Analysis</h3>
                    <p>
                        Developers can identify slow operations and areas of the
                        application that may require optimisation.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div>
                <span class="number">02</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Error Detection</h3>
                    <p>
                        Exceptions and failed requests can be identified and investigated
                        before they significantly affect users.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div>
                <span class="number">03</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Usage Insights</h3>
                    <p>
                        Custom events can provide information about how application
                        features are being used.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>4. Security Considerations</h2>

        <p>
            Telemetry must be implemented responsibly because application monitoring
            systems may process information about users, requests and system activity.
            Developers should avoid recording passwords, authentication secrets and
            unnecessary personally identifiable information within application logs.
        </p>

        <p>
            Security monitoring should form part of the broader risk-management process
            of an organisation. The NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 provides guidance
            for organisations to understand, assess, prioritise and communicate
            cybersecurity risks (Pascoe, Quinn &amp; Scarfone, 2024). Monitoring and
            logging contribute to this objective by improving visibility into events
            occurring within an information system.
        </p>

        <div class="warning-card">
            <h3>Telemetry Data Should Not Include</h3>
            <p>
                Passwords, authentication tokens, financial information or unnecessary
                sensitive personal information should not be recorded as normal
                application telemetry.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>5. Application of Research to SmartX</h2>

        <p>
            The research directly influences the implementation of SmartX. The
            application provides a dedicated telemetry area where operational
            information can be viewed rather than leaving monitoring as an external
            concept that is disconnected from the software.
        </p>

        <p>
            Incorporating telemetry into SmartX demonstrates an understanding that
            application development continues after the implementation of core
            functionality. A production-ready system should provide mechanisms for
            identifying errors, observing performance and understanding significant
            application events.
        </p>

        <p>
            The use of Blazor also allows the telemetry interface to remain consistent
            with the rest of the application. Reusable Razor components, C# code and
            the existing navigation structure can be used to expose monitoring
            information without introducing an entirely separate front-end technology.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>6. Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
            Application telemetry is an important component of reliable software
            development because it provides measurable information about application
            health, behaviour and failures. Microsoft Application Insights and
            OpenTelemetry provide technologies that can collect metrics, traces, logs,
            exceptions and custom application events.
        </p>

        <p>
            For SmartX, telemetry strengthens the application by supporting performance
            monitoring, fault detection and informed maintenance decisions. However,
            monitoring must be implemented together with appropriate security and
            privacy practices. Combining Blazor, telemetry and cybersecurity principles
            therefore contributes towards a more maintainable, observable and secure
            application.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="report-section references">
        <h2>References</h2>

        <div class="reference-item">
            <span>[1]</span>
            <p>
                Microsoft. (2026a). <em>ASP.NET Core Blazor.</em>
                Microsoft Learn.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reference-item">
            <span>[2]</span>
            <p>
                Microsoft. (2026b). <em>Enable OpenTelemetry in Application Insights.</em>
                Azure Monitor, Microsoft Learn.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reference-item">
            <span>[3]</span>
            <p>
                Pascoe, C., Quinn, S. &amp; Scarfone, K. (2024).
                <em>The NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0.</em>
                National Institute of Standards and Technology.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>

